WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
A campaign is ready. Is it ready to launch?
Follow Sarah in compliance and Alex in marketing as one affiliate campaign moves from risky copy to a defensible decision.
Actual product interactions · Edited walkthrough · No audio needed

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Start with the work that needs attention.
Sarah opens a prioritized queue: escalations, distribution holds, and overdue reviews.
THROUGHPUT: Reduce time spent reconstructing status from spreadsheets and email.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
One campaign. All its context.
Search brings Alex’s affiliate campaign into focus. Ownership, channel, version, and due date travel together.
DESIGN CHOICE: A clear next action beats another inbox.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:27.000
“Guaranteed approval” is a problem.
Sarah sees the exact creative beside two advisory findings. The partner’s destination copy is part of the review too.
EDGE CASE: A safe-looking ad can lead to a misleading landing page.

5
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Feedback should be actionable.
Sarah explains what must change: remove the promise, qualify eligibility, and clarify credit inquiries. Approval stays blocked.
PRODUCT THINKING: Specific feedback reduces avoidable revision loops.

6
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.000
The next move belongs to Alex.
The campaign changes state. Alex can read the feedback and revise the submitted creative.
OWNERSHIP: One accountable owner; one visible handoff.

7
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Change the copy. Keep the scope aligned.
Alex revises both the advertisement and captured landing-page copy. The new wording qualifies approval and inquiry claims.
EDGE CASE: Changing only the ad is insufficient when the destination still overpromises.

8
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Version 2 starts a fresh review.
The previous copy and review evidence remain available. New creative invalidates earlier evidence and sign-offs.
AUDITABILITY: No approval silently follows changed creative.

9
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:05.000
The author cannot approve the work.
Alex can submit the revision, but cannot approve it. The decision belongs to an independent compliance reviewer.
SEPARATION OF DUTIES: These are local workflow identities, not authenticated accounts.

10
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:12.000
No flags does not mean cleared.
Sarah resumes the review. The rewritten creative has no automated flags, but evidence and human checks are still required.
HUMAN JUDGMENT: Pre-checks assist reviewers; they do not provide legal clearance.

11
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Show the basis for the decision.
Sarah records the product terms, source reference, and validity date. Evidence belongs to this creative version.
EVIDENCE QUALITY: The source is a manual reference, not an automatically verified document.

12
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Human review still matters.
Verify terms, substantiate claims, and check the audience and distribution scope.
ACCOUNTABILITY: Each completed check enters the activity history.

13
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Human review still matters.
Verify terms, substantiate claims, and check the audience and distribution scope.
ACCOUNTABILITY: Each completed check enters the activity history.

14
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Human review still matters.
Verify terms, substantiate claims, and check the audience and distribution scope.
ACCOUNTABILITY: Each completed check enters the activity history.

15
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:35.000
A small date error. A meaningful safeguard.
Sarah first chooses December 31. The evidence expires December 15, so approval remains disabled.
EDGE CASE: An approval cannot outlive its supporting evidence.

16
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Now the decision is supported.
Sarah shortens the approval period, records her rationale, and completes the required checks.
REVIEW GATE: Version, evidence, policy, scope, and reviewer must all align.

17
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Launch ready. For this exact version.
The approval stores an exportable snapshot of the creative, evidence, scope, policy, reviewer, and rationale.
PRODUCT THINKING: “Approved” is a recorded decision, not a permanent label.

18
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Then the rules change.
A new eligibility requirement affects personal-loan advertising. Sarah opens the versioned policy library.
LIFECYCLE: Compliance continues after the first approval.

19
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:04.000
See the impact before publishing.
The policy update identifies two approved campaigns that will need re-review. Sarah documents what changed.
CHANGE MANAGEMENT: Make the consequences visible before confirming.

20
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Yesterday’s approval is not today’s clearance.
Alex’s campaign moves to a distribution hold because it was reviewed under the previous policy version.
EDGE CASE: Policy changes remove launch clearance without rewriting history.

21
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Stop distribution. Keep the evidence.
Sarah records why the placement must stop while disclosures are corrected and independently reviewed again.
CONTROL: Revocation requires lead authority and a meaningful reason.

22
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The whole story is still here.
The activity chain connects feedback, revision, evidence, approval, policy change, and revocation.
AUDIT TRAIL: A reviewer can explain what happened and why.

23
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Revoke the clearance, not the record.
The original approval snapshot is still exportable, along with the earlier creative version and its review evidence.
RETENTION: Historical decisions stay distinct from current launch readiness.

24
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Manage capacity and distribution risk.
The overview separates open reviews from approval holds and shows reviewer workload and descriptive cycle-time metrics.
MEASUREMENT: Demo metrics are not proof of a throughput improvement.

25
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:51.000
When Sarah is busy, ownership can move.
A reasoned handoff sends another campaign to Jamie. Reviewer attestations reset; evidence and history remain.
EDGE CASE: The new reviewer must verify the work, not inherit unchecked confidence.

26
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Automation will miss things.
Jamie adds a manual finding about disclosure prominence—something a simple text pre-check cannot assess.
HUMAN OVERSIGHT: The workflow supports reviewer judgment beyond the rule engine.

27
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Bring your team. Bring your campaign.
Priya and Jordan are custom local identities. Anyone can test the workflow with their own names, content, and review ownership.
SELF-SERVE: No invitations are sent. Data remains in this browser.

28
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Paste it. Import it. Put it through review.
Import a .txt or .md file, or paste campaign copy. Choose a reviewer and capture the actual distribution scope.
SAFE IMPORT: Files are read locally. Invalid files are rejected; existing copy is not silently overwritten.

29
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Your content. The same safeguards.
Priya’s imported campaign is assigned to Jordan. A custom reviewer follows the same evidence, checklist, and approval rules as the sample team.
EXTENSIBILITY: This is a working intake flow, not a fixed sequence of demo screens.

30
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Two tabs must not overwrite each other.
A change in one tab warns the other. Stale writes are blocked until the workspace is refreshed; typed form drafts remain available.
PRODUCTION THINKING: Web Locks and revision checks prevent accidental lost updates within one browser.

31
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:41.000
Faster reviews. Clearer accountability.
Try your own campaign in the live app. Paste copy or import a .txt/.md file, then choose an independent reviewer.
Browser-local prototype · No real authentication or shared backend: Production requires server-side roles, durable storage, and protected audit retention.
